WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.493
Effort: the first trade.

2
00:00:01.773 --> 00:00:03.309
Effort ( output_config.

3
00:00:03.589 --> 00:00:09.456
effort ) tells the model how much work to put in: how deeply to think and how many tool calls to make.

4
00:00:09.736 --> 00:00:13.917
Current Claude models accept low, medium, high, xhigh, and max.

5
00:00:14.197 --> 00:00:18.443
Defaults differ by model, which is a trap when you migrate: Opus 5.

6
00:00:18.723 --> 00:00:20.195
5 and Haiku 5.

7
00:00:20.475 --> 00:00:23.120
5 default to medium, while Sonnet 5.

8
00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:24.893
5 defaults to high.

9
00:00:25.173 --> 00:00:26.795
Set it explicitly per route.

10
00:00:27.075 --> 00:00:29.315
Effort is a real trade, so measure it.

11
00:00:29.595 --> 00:00:31.963
Anthropic’s published runs of Opus 5.

12
00:00:32.243 --> 00:00:36.253
5 on a coding benchmark show the shape: low solved 87.

13
00:00:36.533 --> 00:00:38.560
4 percent at about $0.

14
00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:41.187
11 per task, medium 92.

15
00:00:41.467 --> 00:00:43.067
8 percent at $0.

16
00:00:43.347 --> 00:00:45.096
21, high 95.

17
00:00:45.376 --> 00:00:47.147
3 percent at $0.

18
00:00:47.427 --> 00:00:49.709
29, and xhigh 96.

19
00:00:49.989 --> 00:00:51.845
7 percent at $0.

20
00:00:52.125 --> 00:00:53.149
74.

21
00:00:53.429 --> 00:00:59.211
On research and knowledge work the guide reports much flatter curves, where lower effort gives up little.

22
00:00:59.491 --> 00:01:01.411
Your curve belongs to your workload.

23
00:01:01.691 --> 00:01:11.995
When you have a cheap failure signal (tests, a validator, a checker), a useful pattern is to run everything at low effort and re-run only the failures at a higher one.

24
00:01:12.275 --> 00:01:17.672
In the same published runs this reached about 97 percent solved for about $0.

25
00:01:17.952 --> 00:01:20.405
17 per task, against 95.

26
00:01:20.685 --> 00:01:22.349
3 percent for $0.

27
00:01:22.629 --> 00:01:24.827
29 running everything at high.

28
00:01:25.107 --> 00:01:37.949
Two caveats: price in the checker and the extra wall-clock time on failures, and sweep each effort level in a separate session, because changing top-level effort mid-conversation invalidates the message cache.
